description:
Non-gating follow-ups to CQ-R01 (protected installed runtime selection), moved out of CQ-R13 (task-f4811a72) by the human's decision in the architect REPL on 2026-10-30. They harden the IDE and client side of the protected (strong) profile:
- persistent daemon provenance across IDE restarts;
- a persistent downgrade pin with a human confirmation;
- replacement of an unverified running daemon;
- the Windows Restart Daemon supervisor race;
- the daemon.json binding threat-model decision;
- VS Code parity.
They do NOT gate CQ-R isolation certification. verified-local stays unavailable.

architectureNotes:
Source: the architect note on CQ-R13 from planning CQ-R01 (2026-10-24, architect-26ba32c3) lists seven follow-ups. Follow-up 6, wrapper fallbacks under strong, stays in CQ-R as CQ-R13c1/R13c2, which add the MOE_RUNTIME_PROTECTED=1 marker set by the JetBrains launcher. The other six are this epic's rows.

R01's design (Serena memory decision-protected-runtime-selection):
- a strict fail-closed profile read;
- a JVM-session downgrade pin;
- installed-only daemon, wrapper and proxy selection;
- spawn-record provenance (pid + startInstant, or the direct parent) checked in doConnect.
Any state these rows persist lives outside the repository, in the IDE system directory, with owner-only access. JetBrains is primary: implement and test it first, and VS Code follows. Kotlin tests run from WSL through cmd.exe gradlew.bat, because there is no Linux JDK; CQ-R01's verification records the exact command.
